  "body": [
    { "line_index": 0, "verse": "150:3", "verse_number_printed": "3", "verse_marker": "†", "text": "† Prayſe ye him in the ſound of trumpet: prayſe ye him on", "ref_letters_present": ["e","f","g"] },
    { "line_index": 1, "verse": "150:3", "text": "pſalter, and harpe." },
    { "line_index": 2, "verse": "150:4", "verse_number_printed": "4", "verse_marker": "†", "text": "† Prayſe ye him on timbrel and quire: prayſe ye him on", "ref_letters_present": ["g","h"] },
    { "line_index": 3, "verse": "150:4", "text": "ſtringes, and organ." },
    { "line_index": 4, "verse": "150:5", "verse_number_printed": "5", "verse_marker": "†", "text": "† Prayſe ye him on wel ſounding cymbals : prayſe ye him on" },
    { "line_index": 5, "verse": "150:6", "verse_number_printed": "6", "verse_marker": "†", "text": "cymbales of iubilation : † let euerie ſpirit prayſe our Lord.", "ref_letters_present": ["i"], "note": "v6 begins mid-line at '† let euerie'; second '†' marks v6" },
    { "line_index": 6, "verse": "150:6", "text": "Alleluia." }
  ],
  "apparatus": [
    { "role": "annotation", "ref_letter": "e", "anchor_verse": "150:3", "typeface": "italic-vv", "text": "Out of this your great and infinite deſire, let your tongues ſound and ſing diuine praiſes, as wel vvith voice, as muſical inſtruments." },
    { "role": "annotation", "ref_letter": "f", "anchor_verse": "150:3", "typeface": "italic-vv", "text": "VVherof ſix moſt vſual in the Tabernacle and Temple vvere theſe: Trumpet, Pſalter, Harpe, Timbrel, Organ and Cymbal." },
    { "role": "annotation", "ref_letter": "g", "anchor_verse": "150:4", "typeface": "italic-vv", "text": "By the vvay the Pſalmiſt interpoſeth agane tvvo eſpecial thinges, vvhich make perfect harmonie, vvithout vvhich no inſtrument is gratful to God; Vnitie amongſt his ſeruants, ſignified by the Quire of conſonant voices:" },
    { "role": "annotation", "ref_letter": "h", "anchor_verse": "150:4", "typeface": "italic-vv", "text": "and mortification of paſſions, ſignified by Stringes, vvhich are made of dead beaſtes bovvels." },
    { "role": "annotation", "ref_letter": "i", "anchor_verse": "150:6", "typeface": "italic-vv", "text": "Man created of corruptible bodie and immortal ſoule, is finally admoniſhed to praiſe our Lord, ouer and aboue the praiſes of al other corporal creatures; vvho alſo is more eſpecially bond therto then Angels, becauſe God hath voutſaffed to make himſelfe Man, to redeme man that vvas loſt by ſinne, and to endevv him vvith nevv grace, and ſo bring him to euerlaſting glorie, vvhere vvith holie Angels, men alſo for euer & euer ſhal praiſe our Lord, vvith hart, voice, and iubilation of ſpirite, ſinging as the Pſalmiſt concludeth, Alleluia." },
    { "role": "annotation_block", "heading": "ANNOTATIONS. PSALME CL.", "typeface": "roman-w", "text": "S. Auguſtin in the concluſion of his Enarrations, or Sermons vpon the Pſalmes, explicateth a myſterie in the number of an hundred and fiſtie, ſignifying the concord of the two Teſtaments. For in the old teſtament they kept the Sabbath, which is the ſeuenth day : in the new we kepe our Lords day, after the ſabbath, that is, the eight : which ſeuen and eight ( making ſiſtene ) multiplied by tenne, ſignifying the Law of tenne commandments, riſe vnto 150. Againe ſeuen multiplied by ſeuen make 49. wherto one (to witte the eight) being added make fiſtie, which multiplied by three, ſignifying the B Trinitie, make 150. Neither ſemed it without cauſe to this great Doctor, that the firſt fiſtie end with a Pſalme of Pænance, crauing mercie & remiſſion of ſinnes: the ſecond with Mercie and Iuſtice, which God ioyneth in the Redemption, Iuſtification, and Saluation of men : the laſt with Diuine Praiſes, ſignifying, that by condemning ſinnes in our ſelues, through Gods mercie we may be iuſtified, and ſo beginne in this life, which is to be perfected in the next, to praiſe our Lord, as S. Paul admoniſheth with Pſalmes, Hymnes, and Spiritual Songues. Concluding with the tvvo verſes, appointed by S. Damaſus Pope, to be added in the end of al Pſalmes, and is obſerued euer ſince his time by tradition in the vvhole Church:" },
    { "role": "gloria", "typeface": "roman-display", "text": "Glorie to the Father, and to the Sonne, and to the Holie Ghoſt: As it was in the beginning, and now, and euer, into worldes of worldes [in eternitie vvithout end] Amen.", "note": "roman display uses real w (was/now/worldes); the bracketed editorial insertion '[in eternitie vvithout end]' is italic → vv." }
  ],
  "marginalia": [
    { "role": "annotation_key", "position": "left_margin", "anchor": "ANNOTATIONS para 1", "text": "The number of Pſalmes ſignifieth the agrement of the old and nevv Teſtament." },
    { "role": "annotation_key", "position": "left_margin", "anchor": "ANNOTATIONS para 2", "text": "Three fifeties ſignifie Penance. Mercie vvith iuſtice, and Praiſes of God." },
    { "role": "annotation_key", "position": "left_margin", "anchor": "Gloria Patri", "text": "Gloria Patri: added by tradition." },
